      ],
      "revId": "1d3d902d4420b6ad133403b8b9430c1537dad521",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a608666f_5d713c5b",
        "filename": "security/selinux/include/policycap.h",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 17,
      "author": {
        "id": 1561651
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-08-12T01:14:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Without the new capability, we would have to put the extended permissions behind the current permissions. That is, for instance, \n\nallowxperm d self:netlink_audit_socket nlmsg_read { RTM_GETLINK };\nallowxperm d self:netlink_audit_socket nlmsg_write { RTM_SETLINK };\n\ninstead of \n\nallowxperm d self:netlink_audit_socket nlmsg { RTM_GETLINK RTM_SETLINK };\n\n\nSee also the list in the commit message on why I think this is a better approach.\n\nSince this is changing which permission is mapped to which action (i.e., all nlmsg_send are now mapped to `nlmsg`), then it needs to be put behind a capability / flag. There was a similar behaviour change recently with POLICYDB_CAP_USERSPACE_INITIAL_CONTEXT.",
      "parentUuid": "6652f60e_9bab61ca",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 17,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 17,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "1d3d902d4420b6ad133403b8b9430c1537dad521",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
